<body>
  <a href="#main" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>
  <header>
    <nav aria-label="Site navigation">
      <a href="/">← StackPilot AI</a>
      <a href="/tools/software-cost-calculator/">Cost calculator</a>
      <a href="/tools/requirements-checklist/">Requirements checklist</a>
      <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow">Buyer education · prelaunch guide</p>
    <h1>SaaS total cost of ownership: what to compare before you buy</h1>
    <p class="lead">A software subscription is more than its advertised monthly headline. The total cost of ownership is what your team expects to pay across a defined period, including user seats, add-ons, implementation, and other charges that may apply.</p>
    <p class="notice"><strong>Independent educational guide:</strong> The worked prices below are entirely hypothetical, not a quote from any vendor. No provider pricing or product capability has been independently verified in this guide. A lower cost does not mean better fit.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/tools/software-cost-calculator/">Compare your own estimates</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/tools/requirements-checklist/">List your must-have features</a>
    </div>
    <section aria-labelledby="toc">
      <h2 id="toc">On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#recurring">Recurring costs to include</a></li>
        <li><a href="#one-time">Implementation and migration expenses</a></li>
        <li><a href="#example">A twelve-month hypothetical comparison</a></li>
        <li><a href="#contract">Contract, renewal and cancellation checks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#questions">Questions to ask the provider</a></li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section id="recurring" class="panel">
      <h2>1. Recurring costs: billing period, seats and add-ons</h2>
      <p><strong>Base subscription:</strong> Record the charge per billing period, and distinguish a true month-to-month contract from a lower rate advertised with an annual commitment. Compare equivalent billing periods and account for the number of months you expect to use the service.</p>
      <p><strong>Seats and permissions:</strong> Some plans charge by user, agent, administrator, or other billable seat. Check whether the seats you need are included, whether inactive users still count, and whether permission restrictions require a higher tier. Do not assume every vendor uses the same seat model.</p>
      <p><strong>Add-ons and extra capacity:</strong> Report packs, integrations, additional contacts, storage, automation execution, API capacity, or premium support may be optional or included depending on the specific plan. Collect a written breakdown rather than adding marketing-page bullet points as guaranteed features.</p>
      <p><strong>Variable usage and overage:</strong> Request the measurement method, reset date, alerts, usage caps and charge schedule. A simple fixed-cost estimate may be too low if usage grows; create a separate high-usage scenario if needed.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="one-time" class="panel">
      <h2>2. One-time costs: setup, training and migration</h2>
      <p>A migration can create expenses even when the new subscription looks cheaper. Evaluate data export and cleanup, mapping custom fields, importing records, rebuilding integrations, training staff, testing automations, and overlapping old/new subscriptions during a transition.</p>
      <p>Check who does the migration, whether it is included in the contract, and whether important relationships or attachments can be exported again if you leave. Keep internal employee time separate from vendor invoices if the two scenarios require different amounts of effort.</p>
      <p>When migration is uncertain, record a low and high estimate and repeat the calculation. One-off costs should not be mistaken for monthly recurring fees.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="example" class="panel">
      <h2>3. Worked example: a hypothetical twelve-month comparison</h2>
      <p>Imagine two imaginary software subscriptions serving a five-person team. All entries below are <strong>invented illustrations in USD</strong>, not a quote, offer, or statement about a real company. They use the same twelve-month horizon and currency.</p>
      <div class="scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Hypothetical software cost comparison table, scroll horizontally to see all columns">
        <table>
          <caption class="muted" style="text-align:left;padding-bottom:12px">Illustrative buyer-entered prices, not vendor pricing</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Cost component</th><th scope="col">Option A</th><th scope="col">Option B</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Monthly base</th><td class="amount">{money(example.optionA.flatMonthlyMinor)}</td><td class="amount">{money(example.optionB.flatMonthlyMinor)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Monthly cost per seat</th><td class="amount">{money(example.optionA.seatMonthlyMinor)}</td><td class="amount">{money(example.optionB.seatMonthlyMinor)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Seats</th><td>{example.optionA.seats}</td><td>{example.optionB.seats}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Other monthly costs</th><td class="amount">{money(example.optionA.extrasMonthlyMinor)}</td><td class="amount">{money(example.optionB.extrasMonthlyMinor)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">One-time setup</th><td class="amount">{money(example.optionA.oneTimeFeeMinor)}</td><td class="amount">{money(example.optionB.oneTimeFeeMinor)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Calculated monthly recurring</th><td class="amount">{money(example.optionA.recurringMonthlyMinor)}</td><td class="amount">{money(example.optionB.recurringMonthlyMinor)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Calculated 12-month total</th><td class="amount"><strong>{money(example.optionA.totalMinor)}</strong></td><td class="amount"><strong>{money(example.optionB.totalMinor)}</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The calculation is <strong>(monthly base + per-seat charge × seats + other monthly fees) × months + one-time setup</strong>. In this invented example, Option B's entered cost is <strong>{money(example.differenceMinor)}</strong> lower across twelve months.</p>
      <p><strong>Important:</strong> The cheaper entered estimate is not necessarily a better purchase. The example omits taxes, discounts, currency exchange, usage overages and future price changes. Compare feature requirements and your actual contracts before deciding.</p>
      <p><a href="/tools/software-cost-calculator/">Replace the fictional amounts with your own estimates in the private calculator.</a></p>
    </section>
    <section id="contract" class="panel">
      <h2>4. Contract length, renewal and cancellation</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Pricing and renewal</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Is the advertised monthly rate tied to annual billing or payment in advance?</li>
            <li>Does the introductory discount end at renewal? Which rate applies next?</li>
            <li>Can you add or remove seats in the middle of a billing period?</li>
            <li>What happens if a quota is exceeded—blocking, automatic upgrades, or overage charges?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Exit and data portability</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Is there a minimum commitment or cancellation notice period?</li>
            <li>Which data, configurations, history and attachments can you export?</li>
            <li>Are migrations, integrations, support, or data retention billed separately?</li>
            <li>Where will your data be hosted, and how do privacy and regional requirements affect the chosen plan?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="questions" class="panel">
      <h2>5. Questions to send before signing</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Can you confirm a written total for our seats, usage, add-ons and the full billing period?</li>
        <li>Which fees vary with storage, traffic, records, messages, API requests or support?</li>
        <li>What are the tax and currency rules for our country, and how might a cross-border payment affect the amount?</li>
        <li>How do upgrades, seat removals, renewal and cancellation affect the invoice?</li>
        <li>Which features in our requirements checklist are included in the precise plan being quoted?</li>
        <li>What steps and costs are involved in export, implementation, training and future migration away?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Save the provider's written answers alongside the date, selected plan, location, and any assumptions. Revisit them before renewal. Do not treat an outdated web pricing page as a binding quote.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/tools/software-cost-calculator/">Compare entered costs</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/tools/requirements-checklist/">Prepare vendor questions</a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>What this guide cannot determine</h2>
      <p>It does not measure real-world reliability, implementation difficulty, support quality, legal terms, or the suitability of a product. The calculator uses only the figures that you provide. Review our <a href="/methodology/">research methodology</a> for the status of independent product verification.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer><a href="/">StackPilot AI</a> · <a href="/privacy/">Privacy notes</a> · <a href="/disclosure/">Commercial disclosure</a></footer>
</body>
</html>
